==Status Report: 2026-10-11 (research / plan — no implementation)==
Big-picture answer first: yes, the three-stage design is the right shape, with one
important correction — the existing normalization pipeline *already transliterates
non-ASCII to ASCII* (Normalizer stage 1), and speech is delivered as a plain
`android.speech.tts.TextToSpeech.speak(String)` with no per-word locale/phoneme
hints. So stages 2–3 can't just "wrap a word in SSML" and hope the engine respects
it through the plain-text speak() path; they require either (a) switching to
SSML/full API params for flagged utterances, or (b) producing a *pronunciation
respelling in plain ASCII* as the output (e.g. IPA → a phonetically-respelled word
the engine will say acceptably). Both are viable; (b) is far lower-risk and keeps
the app's dependency-light "plain text to engine" model. See stage notes below.

Stage 1 — which words are "foreign" / dubious to pronounce as English:
  • Android has NO built-in "is this word in English" API. There is a system
    `Locale`-based spell-checker service (framework `TextServicesManager`) but it
    is user-dependent, slow, and not reliable for this in practice.
  • Practical, dependency-light heuristic: keep a bundled English wordlist
    (SCOWL / wordfreq top-N, a few MB but compressible — or a hashed/Bloom set) and
    flag tokens not in it. This avoids N network calls entirely for stage 1.
    Cost: one-time APK growth (~1–3 MB compressed), matching the "small ish" but
    "might be worth it" tradeoff you already anticipated.
  • Cheaper alternative to evaluate: heuristics only (non-ASCII chars, rare
    diacritic patterns, title-case-in-sentence, un-English n-grams). Far smaller,
    but more false positives/negatives.
  • Recommendation: bundled wordlist Bloom/frozen set + a small "always-English"
    whitelist of common noise words. No network for stage 1.

Stage 2 — foreign → IPA / pronunciation:
  • Wiktionary/Wikidata via API is the right instinct. Wikidata has lexemes with
    IPA; Wiktionary dumps have IPA transcripts. Both are queryable in *batch*:
    Wikidata SPARQL / Wikibase `EntitySearch`+`wbeditentity`-adjacent reads, or the
    Wiktionary REST `*` batch via a single `search` + `parse` call, can cover many
    words per request (addressing your "50–150 single calls" concern).
  • Rate limits: Wikimedia asks for a User-Agent and moderate QPS; batching 50
    words into one request (or a few) is the key lever. A local LRU cache
    keyed on the word collapses repeats across articles and across sessions.
  • Fallback when a word has no IPA: a language-detection guess (e.g. detect the
    source language from the word/context) → generic mapping table, or leave as-is.
  • Dependency: nothing heavy — plain HTTP (OkHttp already likely present) against
    Wikimedia endpoints. No dictionary SDK needed.

Stage 3 — feed the pronunciation back into speech:
  • SSML: Android's TextToSpeech does surface SSML only for a *narrow* set of
    engines/configurations. `speak()` does NOT accept inline `<phoneme>`/IPA
    reliably (IPA support is essentially absent from the standard engine surface;
    some engines accept `<phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="...">` only in
    `synthesizeToFile`/full-vocab files). So wrapping in SSML is a red herring for
    this app's current plain-text model and would break the "only Cookero/Pocket"
    requirement anyway (they don't all parse SSML identically).
  • The robust, engine-agnostic path: convert the IPA back to an ASCII
    *pronunciation respelling* in Normalizer stage(s), so we emit e.g. "Versailles"
    → "vair-SIGH", joined to the surrounding text. This uses the exact pipeline
    already in place (an ordered transform), needs no SSML, and works on every
    engine including the two named. This is the recommended approach.
  • If full-IPA is ever wanted for an engine that supports it, that is a *separate*
    opt-in knob on the per-engine params path (`Bundle` extras or a vocabulary
    file) — keep it out of v1.

Report summary (difficulty / dependencies / network):
  • Stage 1: easy-to-medium, offline, ~1–3 MB compressed bundle, zero network.
  • Stage 2: medium, HTTP-only, batched Wikimedia calls, needs a local cache +
    polite UA; network cost dominated by first-seen words only.
  • Stage 3: medium, pure-Kotlin in Normalizer (a new earlier stage before
    transliterate/diacritic-folding, or a precise token-level replace before stage
    1), zero dependency, zero network.
  • Overall APK cost: modest (wordlist + no new SDKs). No per-article API-call
    storms if stage 1 is offline and stage 2 is batched + cached.

Open questions for you before any "go":
  1. Confirm preference: pronunciation *respelling* (ASCII, engine-agnostic — my
     recommendation) vs. SSML/IPA for only some engines?
  2. Acceptable bundled wordlist size (~1–3 MB) for stage 1 offline detection?
  3. Batch: is an upfront "fetch pronunciation for all unknown words after article
     load, in one or two requests" acceptable (small delay on first listen), vs.
     lazy inline?

Still a research project — nothing implemented, no code written.
